CONTEXT FROM TEXTBOOK:
This figure looks straight down the axis of a chair-form cyclohexane ring in two matched models that color-code the two kinds of hydrogen position. On the left is a space-filling model: a compact dark gray core of ring carbons is surrounded by six large red spheres and six large blue spheres packed against one another, the red ones sitting toward the center of the disc and the blue ones fanning outward around the rim, the two colors alternating as the eye travels around the ring. A magenta arrow at the upper left points to a red sphere and is labeled "Axial"; a blue arrow at the top points to a blue sphere and is labeled "Equatorial". On the right the same view appears as a ball-and-stick model, the six dark gray carbons forming a hexagonal outline, with red spheres projecting from alternating carbons and blue spheres held outward from every carbon on thin pale rods. Separating the two positions by color rather than by perspective lets the reader count them directly, establishing that each ring carbon bears exactly one axial and one equatorial hydrogen and that each face carries three of each in alternating order.

PROJECTION TYPE: perspective
Use the scaffold's true perspective projection after building all geometry. Use this only when the source has real vanishing-point convergence or distance-based size shrinkage; then depth should visibly affect apparent size.

ELEMENTS TO RECREATE IN 3D:
  - Chair-conformation cyclohexane ring of six dark-gray carbon atoms (alternating up/down puckering)
  - Six axial hydrogens, red, one per carbon, pointing parallel to the ring's C3 axis (three up, three down, alternating)
  - Six equatorial hydrogens, blue, one per carbon, splayed outward roughly in the ring plane
  - Space-filling (CPK) model panel: overlapping van der Waals spheres — compact dark-gray carbon core, red spheres clustered toward the disc center, large blue spheres fanning around the rim, red/blue alternating around the ring
  - Ball-and-stick model panel: small dark-gray carbon spheres forming a hexagonal outline, thin pale-gray/blue rods for C–C ring bonds and C–H bonds, red spheres on alternating carbons (axial, projecting toward viewer/away), blue spheres held outward from every carbon
  - Magenta arrow from upper-left pointing at a red sphere, with magenta text label 'Axial'
  - Blue arrow from top pointing at a blue rim sphere, with blue text label 'Equatorial'
  - Neutral white background, two side-by-side panels (space-filling left, ball-and-stick right)

GEOMETRY NOTES (world-space scale, color palette, and any formula/value needed for correctness — build to these so sizes, zoom, and colors match the source; do not guess dimensions the notes already specify):
Use 1 Å = 1 world unit. Cyclohexane chair, exact coordinates: six carbons at radius r = 1.46 from the ring axis (the axis = world +Y after the top-down camera, but build the ring in the XZ plane with pucker along Y), angles 0°,60°,...,300°, y = +0.25 for even-indexed carbons and y = −0.25 for odd-indexed (C–C 1.54 Å, C–C–C 111°). Axial C–H: length 1.09 along +Y from the up-puckered carbons and −Y from the down-puckered ones (exactly parallel to the ring axis). Equatorial C–H: length 1.09, directed radially outward from the axis and tilted ~19–20° opposite in sign to that carbon's axial direction (up-puckered carbon → equatorial tilts slightly down). Space-filling panel: van der Waals radii C 1.70, H 1.20, spheres interpenetrating so the gray carbon core is barely visible between the red/blue caps; red axial caps project toward/away from the viewer so they read as the inner ring of the disc, blue equatorial spheres form the outer rim — overall disc ≈ 6.5 units across. Ball-and-stick panel: C sphere r 0.36, H sphere r 0.24, rods r 0.075; ring spans ≈ 4.5 units across. Layout: in Side-by-side mode place the space-filling model centered at x = −4.6 and the ball-and-stick model at x = +4.6 (same size relationship as the source: space-filling disc visually a bit larger than the ball-and-stick hexagon); single-model modes center the model at origin. Colors: carbon dark slate gray #4B5053 with lighter specular highlight; axial hydrogen brick red #B8352A (space-filling) / #C0392B (ball-and-stick), with a glossy near-white specular dot upper-left of each sphere as in the source; equatorial hydrogen sky blue #46A8DC; bond rods pale gray-blue #C6D4DC with slightly darker outline; background white. Label 'Axial' in magenta #E0218A at upper-left with a magenta curved arrow pointing down-right onto an upper-left red sphere; label 'Equatorial' in blue #1C9AD6 at top with a blue arrow angled down-left onto the top blue rim sphere; labels attach to whichever model is leftmost/visible and hide when their hydrogen set is filtered out.

DISCRETE CONTROLS (implement every one of these in #ui, each must work independently):
[
  {
    "id": "orbitRing",
    "type": "orbit",
    "teaches": "Rotating away from the down-the-axis view reveals that the flat-looking hexagon is really a chair: the red axial hydrogens all stand parallel to the ring axis (alternating up and down) while the blue equatorial hydrogens splay outward near the ring's belt."
  },
  {
    "id": "selectCarbon",
    "type": "click",
